 [reaction.registers]
-lv1 = "Draw a cross on paper, stand the beaker on it, and start the clock when the acid goes in. The liquid slowly turns milky with sulfur until the cross vanishes. Strong solution: 24 seconds. Half as strong: 49. A quarter as strong: 113. Fainter mixture, longer wait — and the clock is the measuring instrument, which is the whole trick of the experiment."
-lv2 = "Taking 0.01 mol/L of suspended sulfur as the point where the cross is hidden, the bench reaches it at 24 s (0.10 mol/L), 49 s (0.05 mol/L) and 113 s (0.025 mol/L). 1/t is the usual stand-in for the rate: 0.0417, 0.0204 and 0.0088 s⁻¹, close to halving each time but not exactly — the successive time ratios are 2.04 and 2.31, not 2.00 and 2.00. At the moment each beaker is read it has used 10%, 20% and 40% of its thiosulfate, and the most dilute one is no longer anywhere near its initial rate."
-lv3 = "The absolute times belong to us and the ratios belong to the chemistry — that distinction is the honest content of this entry. The pre-exponential A = 2.2 × 10⁸ in `kinetics.rs` is calibrated so that 0.05 mol/L at pH 1.7 and 25 °C hides the cross in about forty seconds, which is the range the practical is designed around; it is not a measured Arrhenius A, and the file says so. Everything derived from *comparing* vessels — the order, the temperature ratios, the catalyst factors — is independent of A and survives any recalibration. The exact integrated form is t = −ln(1 − 0.01/c₀)/(k[H⁺]), which with the engine's own k[H⁺] = 4.561 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ puts the crossings at 23.1, 48.9 and 112.0 s and the ratios at 2.12 and 2.29; reading the beaker once a second rounds those to 24, 49 and 113 and distorts the first ratio to 2.04. Nearly a second of quantisation is worth noticing, because a real practical reads its stopwatch to about the same precision. The 0.01 mol/L threshold is also ours — it is the criterion the engine's own regression test uses — and it is not the same thing as the renderer's vocabulary: `appearance` calls a beaker 'cloudy' from a cloudiness of 0.15, which is 0.0075 mol/L of sulfur and arrives about thirteen seconds earlier in the 0.05 mol/L beaker. Each beaker is therefore already being described as 'cloudy' by the time it reaches the criterion, and the two thresholds should not be read as one."
+lv1 = "A stronger mixture may turn cloudy sooner, but waiting cannot supply missing acid. Count the reagents before using a disappearing mark as a timer."
+lv2 = "At most 1 mmol of sulfur can be formed from 2 mmol of represented acid by this stoichiometry. Depletion reduces the rate as that limit is approached. The old constant-acid crossing times are not valid for this finite acid inventory."
+lv3 = "The snapshots at 24, 49 and 113 seconds are observation times, not claimed crossing times. Optical cloudiness thresholds are teaching conventions, not measured universal sulfur scattering laws. The absolute kinetic coefficient remains calibrated; finite acid consumption is now explicit."
 
